# Navigate the robot to a pose, and say WHY when it cannot.
#
# Served by task_runner (syncai_task_runner, SyncAI-Robot-Workspace) on the
# relative name `navigate_to_goal` -> <robot_id>/navigate_to_goal, next to the
# UNCHANGED nav2_msgs/NavigateToPose on <robot_id>/navigate_to_pose: same
# navigator code, same behavior tree (move.xml), same preemption rules; only
# the result and one feedback field differ. Added 2026-10 because the
# backend's MOVE step got ABORTED with an empty std_msgs/Empty result and
# could not tell the operator whether the robot failed to plan or failed to
# drive. The backend migrates from NavigateToPose at its own pace; the old
# action stays until it has.
#
# Field NAMES mirror nav2's NavigateToPose on purpose (goal.pose,
# goal.behavior_tree, the five feedback fields): one navigator template in
# task_runner serves both actions, and it compiles only because the member
# names agree. Rename a field here and that template needs a trait.

# Target pose, in the navigator's global_frame (map).
geometry_msgs/PoseStamped pose
# Path of a BT XML to tick instead of the default move.xml; empty = the
# default. Absolute, or (since 2026-10) a bare file name resolved against
# task_runner's own behavior_trees/ directory: "dock.xml" and "undock.xml"
# are the docking trees (see DockToPose.action), and for those `pose` is the
# DOCK pose -- the trees derive the staging pose from it themselves. A new
# goal sent while one runs is accepted in place (no restart, no replan
# cycle) only if it names the same tree; otherwise it is terminated and the
# running goal continues.
string behavior_tree
---
# Which stage failed, coarsely. Meaningful ONLY with status ABORTED;
# SUCCEEDED and CANCELED carry NONE.
#
# ABORTED + NONE is not a navigation failure. It means the goal was
# superseded by a newer goal on this action (preempted in place: the old goal
# is aborted with a default result), or was rejected before the tree ran --
# the other navigator in task_runner was busy (navigate_to_pose and
# navigate_to_goal share one robot, so one navigation at a time), or the
# requested behavior_tree file could not be loaded. The task_runner log has
# which.
#
# Layout, nav2 Iron's convention, so finer codes slot in without renumbering:
#   0        none
#   1..99    navigator / tree level
#   100..199 the path-following branch (FollowPath -> syncai_controller)
#   200..299 the planning branch (ComputePathToPose -> syncai_planner)
#   300..399 the docking branch (dock.xml / undock.xml only: SetMotionKey ->
#            syncai_driver_manager, DockToPose -> syncai_controller's
#            dock_to_pose; DockToPose.action numbers its own reasons 301..)
# The branch constants are their range's base. A finer reason is base + n
# and a client that switches on the hundreds keeps working. The bases are a
# contract between this file and the backend: never renumber them.
#
# Finer reasons exist for the planning branch since 2026-11: the planner's
# own result (syncai_common/ComputePathToPose) carries a code, and the
# navigator copies it here unchanged -- 201..224 below, the same table as
# ComputePathToPose, ComputePathThroughPoses and NavigateThroughGoals (keep
# the four in step). 200 itself is still what a planning failure with no
# finer reason reads. The path-following branch has none yet: Humble's
# FollowPath result carries no reason, so 1xx is 100 only.
uint16 NONE=0
# The tree failed without a node reporting: an exception inside the tree, or
# a branch whose server never acknowledged the goal (error_msg names it).
uint16 UNKNOWN=1
# The path-following branch failed the tree, after move.xml's own retries:
# blocked with no detour, no progress for movement_time_allowance, or the
# robot pose lost for longer than failure_tolerance.
uint16 FOLLOW_PATH_FAILED=100
# The planning branch failed the tree -- twice, since the tree clears the
# planner's dynamic overlay and retries once before giving up -- with no
# finer reason below: a planner that gave none, or one that threw.
uint16 PLAN_FAILED=200
# The planning branch's finer reasons: ComputePathToPose's table, where each
# is documented with whether the same goal sent again can succeed -- the
# question a client deciding on a retry is asking. In short: permanent are
# 201, 203, 204, 220 (until a map switch), 222 (until the robot is moved),
# 223 (until the zone is edited) and 224; the others may clear by themselves.
uint16 INVALID_PLANNER=201
uint16 TF_ERROR=202
uint16 START_OUTSIDE_MAP=203
uint16 GOAL_OUTSIDE_MAP=204
uint16 START_OCCUPIED=205
uint16 GOAL_OCCUPIED=206
uint16 TIMEOUT=207
uint16 NO_VALID_PATH=208
uint16 NO_VIAPOINTS_GIVEN=209
uint16 MAP_NOT_LOADED=220
uint16 MAP_LOADING=221
uint16 START_IN_KEEPOUT=222
uint16 GOAL_IN_KEEPOUT=223
uint16 DISCONNECTED=224
# The docking branch of behavior_trees/dock.xml or undock.xml failed: the
# gait controller refused a motion-mode switch (the driver's safety lock,
# "LOCKED"), the dock pose was unusable (no orientation, staging distance
# <= 0), or the dock approach / back-out aborted -- the controller's
# DockToPose reason (transform, timeout, lost control) is in error_msg. The
# tree restores RL locomotion before reporting this, so the robot is left in
# the gait the step found it in -- except when only dock.xml's final
# lie-down was refused: the robot is then standing ON the dock, still in MPC,
# and is deliberately not switched to the walking gait. A dock goal can still end with PLAN_FAILED
# / FOLLOW_PATH_FAILED: that is dock.xml's staging leg, i.e. the dock was
# unreachable, not the dock itself. dock and undock share this base because
# the backend knows which tree it sent; error_msg says approach or back-out.
uint16 DOCK_FAILED=300

uint16 error_code
# Human-readable, written for the operator console: a stage sentence with
# the BT node's detail in parentheses. Empty when error_code is NONE.
string error_msg
---
# nav2's five fields, same names, plus one.
geometry_msgs/PoseStamped current_pose
builtin_interfaces/Duration navigation_time
builtin_interfaces/Duration estimated_time_remaining
int16 number_of_recoveries
float32 distance_remaining
# Plans made for this goal beyond the first: 0 while the first path is still
# good. Since 2026-10 move.xml plans only when the path is blocked or the
# goal changed, so this counts detours, which is what an operator watching a
# stalled robot wants to know. Resets with every (pre-empted) goal.
uint16 number_of_replans
# What the navigator is doing right now, for the operator console: one
# English sentence written by the behavior tree at each turn of move.xml --
# planning, following the path, replanning because the path is blocked or the
# goal changed, retrying after the planner or the controller gave up. Empty
# only before the tree has started on the goal. Free text: a client shows it
# and must not switch on it; the result's error_code is the machine-readable
# surface. Without it the console could say only "running" for a goal that
# was in fact detouring round a blocker or about to give up.
string status_msg
